WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:01.199
Vsauce!

00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:05.430
Kevin here, and I haven’t had food or drink
in days.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:12.590
I'm so hungry and I'm so thirsty... and I've
got these Bagel Bites and this bottle of Mountain

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:13.590
Dew.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:19.290
If I’m equally hungry and thirsty, do I
eat or drink?

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.230
Eat or drink?

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.680
Eat or drink?

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:23.990
Eat or drink?

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:24.990
Eat or drink?

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:25.990
Eat or drink?

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:26.990
Eat or drink?!

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:27.990
Eat or drink?

00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:28.990
Eat or drink?

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:29.990
Eat or drink?

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:34.910
And if I sit here paralyzed by indecision
long enough, I will die of starvation AND

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:35.960
dehydration.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:38.810
Well, technically dehydration first.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:39.810
ANYWAY.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:41.270
THAT’S NOT GOOD.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:47.810
This classic decision-theory paradox was created
in the 14th century by Jean Buridan involving

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:56.239
a famished donkey who dies from indecision
and this whole thing is completely irrelevant,

00:00:56.239 --> 00:01:02.989
absurd and has no bearing on our modern, sophisticated,
21st century lives, right?

00:01:02.989 --> 00:01:03.989
WRONG.

00:01:03.989 --> 00:01:07.889
We’re currently faced with one of the most
important choices of our time.

00:01:07.889 --> 00:01:11.020
EVERYTHING hinges on what we decide.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.320
Your future is at stake.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:18.689
And in this period of dissension, friction,
and strife, and everything that’s going

00:01:18.689 --> 00:01:26.049
on we need to band together and elect a President...
of YouTube.

00:01:26.049 --> 00:01:30.840
After months of debates and campaigning, the
people have spoken.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:37.610
Two candidates have emerged: the macabre internet
archaeologist Whang! and the feculent animation

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.340
goblin PsychicPebbles.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:42.680
It is a time for choosing.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:46.950
Let’s meet our candidates.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:55.479
Whang: As your YouTube President, I promise
to restore this website to its former glory

00:01:55.479 --> 00:01:56.479
days.

00:01:56.479 --> 00:02:01.890
Its days of offensive, grotesque, brand-unfriendly
wild west chaos.

00:02:01.890 --> 00:02:07.810
“Malicious insults based on intrinsic attributes,”
that's what this website was built on!

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:08.810
Copyright?

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:09.810
Shmopyright!

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:10.810
C’mon!

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.749
Making fun of *censored* is hilarious!

00:02:12.749 --> 00:02:15.750
Vote for freedom and join the Whang Gang.

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:16.849
Kevin: ALRIGHT!

00:02:16.849 --> 00:02:17.849
Uh.

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:18.849
Thank you, Whang.

00:02:18.849 --> 00:02:22.629
Uh… let’s hear from Pebbles.

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:30.090
Pebbles: As your YouTube President, I pledge
that every YouTuber with over a million subscribers

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.819
must compete in an annual no-holds-barred,
thunderdome-style battle royale where the

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:36.140
winner keeps their channel.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.219
And all the others are deleted.

00:02:38.219 --> 00:02:44.080
So I ask you, for a vote for fairness and
to join the Cult of Pebbles.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.510
Uhhh y’know we have a great selection of
imported cheese and that’s just one of the

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:48.510
perks.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:49.510
I’m not even gonna go on.

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:50.510
But yeah, there’s a lot.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:51.510
That’s just one.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:55.890
Kevin: Fighting to the death sounds bad but
I do like cheese...

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.239
I don’t know who to pick.

00:02:58.239 --> 00:03:03.640
You though, you probably know right away whether
you’re in the lawless Whang Gang or you

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.560
want to be lured in by the Cult of Pebbles.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.930
They’re so different from each other that
the choice is obvious.

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.200
Or you definitely know which one you don’t
want.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.560
Which is GOOD for the candidates!

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:18.560
No.

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:21.310
No, it’s surprisingly... not.

00:03:21.310 --> 00:03:23.590
I’VE GOT GAS.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.719
So I’m gonna open up a gas station.

00:03:25.719 --> 00:03:31.629
Jake’s Pump ‘n’ Dump gas station already
exists near the middle of town, it’s right

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:34.129
next to Taco Bell, obviously so.

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:40.650
I’ll open mine further away -- we’re selling
gas, as long as the prices are similar people

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.499
are just gonna fill up where it’s closest.

00:03:43.499 --> 00:03:48.329
Jake can have these customers, I’ll have
these.

00:03:48.329 --> 00:03:49.760
No problem, right?

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:50.760
...Wrong.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.930
If the two are far apart, and we do pretty
much the same thing, which we do, we’ll

00:03:55.930 --> 00:04:00.319
be splitting the customers in that distance
between us.

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:06.610
So… the smaller that distance, the fewer
customers I lose to the Pump ‘n Dump.

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:13.920
I get everything on my side of town, and I
don’t lose anything between our two gas

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:14.920
stations.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:20.160
And hey, if I run some amazing gas station
sushi promotion, maybe I can even pull some

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.110
customers from barely into Jake’s side of
town.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:33.290
The further I am away, the more people I have
to convince to choose my gas station and not

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:34.360
his.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.850
This is why McDonald’s and Burger King have
basically the same menu with their own spin

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:45.470
and even their logos are both primarily yellow
and red and why fast food places in general

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:48.680
are all typically located near each other.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:54.661
It’s called Hotelling’s Law, outlined
in the 1929 "Stability in Competition" by

00:04:54.661 --> 00:05:00.590
economist Harold Hotelling, stating that it’s
rational for two competitors to be as similar

00:05:00.590 --> 00:05:05.950
as possible to each other while retaining
their own distinct identity.

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:06.950
Cool.

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:07.950
TIME FOR WHANG!

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:12.310
Whang: As your YouTube President, I will bring
back the old five star-rating system.

00:05:12.310 --> 00:05:17.540
Pebbles: Well, as your YouTube President,
I too will bring back the star rating system.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:19.140
But there will be six stars.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.480
Not five!

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.590
On this issue, the two candidates are… pretty
close.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.510
There’s a difference, but it’s not much.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:33.080
Hotelling’s Law happens with voting -- it’s
in a candidate’s interest to be pretty similar

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.910
to their opponent to minimize that loss in
the middle.

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:37.910
HEY!

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:39.190
Do you want an apple or an orange?

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:40.680
Just pick one.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.690
Yeah, that’s not very hard.

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:48.700
You probably like one fruit more than the
other so it’s a simple choice.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:52.170
But do you want this apple or… that apple?

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:55.150
How do you even make that decision?

00:05:55.150 --> 00:05:59.500
You base it on the size of the apple?

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:02.330
The shape of the apple?

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:04.420
Its stem?

00:06:04.420 --> 00:06:12.970
That’s Fredkin’s Paradox: the closer two
choices are, the harder it is to choose.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.970
So at this point I really start to analyze.

00:06:15.970 --> 00:06:16.970
I dig deep.

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:23.240
I stress over little differences and can even
see some that aren’t really there, because

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:28.550
to make a meaningful choice I need there to
be a difference.

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:33.030
And now you’re thinkin’, “This is like
a C. Northcote Parkinson problem.”

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:34.640
And you’re right.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:41.710
In 1957, C. Northcote Parkinson proposed that
the more you analyze something, the more obsessed

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:47.720
you become with the less-relevant details
and… forget the really big stuff.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:53.110
He wrote that, ‘The time spent on any item
of an agenda will be in inverse proportion

00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:55.620
to the sum of money involved.’

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:02.500
“WHERE SHOULD I GO TO COLLEGE!?” is a
question I put some thought into and it cost

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.220
tens of thousands of dollars.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:10.990
“WHAT VIDEO GAME SHOULD I GET?!” is a
question I OBSESS over and that’ll cost

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:13.380
60 bucks at most.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:18.920
I’m trapped in the “Tyranny of Small Decisions”
-- What economist Alfred E. Khan called that

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:24.110
hyperfocus on the tiny details that actually
gets you further from the overall outcome

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.150
you wanted in the first place.

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:32.010
And then Sayre’s Law knocks on my forehead
and says "In any dispute the intensity of

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:38.110
feeling is inversely proportional to the value
of the issues at stake" -- like a couple who’s

00:07:38.110 --> 00:07:43.770
constantly fights over whether the toilet
paper feeds from the front or the back and

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:50.540
never discusses the mounting credit card debt
from buying old PS1 strategy guides off eBay.

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:53.610
I just beat Legend Of Dragoon recently…

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:55.920
It was awesome.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:56.920
So.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:08:00.250
HOW’S THAT PRESIDENT OF YOUTUBE CHOICE GOIN?

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:05.500
Now that I’m in the weeds, I don’t know
who to choose, I’m emotionally invested

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:11.490
in figuring it out, I go deep on less-important
details, and I’m kinda starting to lose

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:12.490
my mind.

00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:14.420
So Alright Alright Wait wait wait.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:15.610
How about this?

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:20.110
Maybe this election is not even about me.

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:24.720
Maybe I want to do what’s best for the world
around me.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:27.270
Think about a bored family in Texas!

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:29.550
It’s Saturday, no one’s doing anything.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:35.650
The dad suggests they all take a trip up to
Abilene an hour away because he thinks the

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:37.610
family will enjoy it.

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:42.970
He knows the drive sucks and he’ll sweat
the whole time, so he’s not too excited,

00:08:42.970 --> 00:08:44.020
but his family?

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:45.900
They’ll love it.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:52.180
He asks his wife and she’s happy to go to
Abilene because she thinks the group wants

00:08:52.180 --> 00:08:53.180
it, too.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:58.280
She asks their son, and he says yes because
he doesn’t want to disappoint his parents.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:02.360
Their daughter doesn’t want to be the only
protest vote, and at this point she would

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:07.670
lose 3-1 anyway, so she enthusiastically says
yes, too.

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:14.170
And that’s how a family of 4 unanimously
voted to take a long, hot trip in a car full

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:18.850
of sweat and Dad’s farts that NONE of them
actually wanted to take.

00:09:18.850 --> 00:09:25.500
That’s Jerry B. Harvey’s “Abilene Paradox”
-- and voting for what no one really wants

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:28.640
happens a lot in selfless democracies.

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:34.400
But here’s my question: Is that just a function
of having only two choices?

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:37.880
Whang or Pebbles, stay home or go to Abilene?

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:41.810
It’s got to get easier when you have more
choices, right?

00:09:41.810 --> 00:09:42.810
Wrong!

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:48.120
Arrow's impossibility theorem shows us that
when voters have 3 or more different options,

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:54.710
there’s just no way to get exactly the right
community-wide outcome even if we rank candidate

00:09:54.710 --> 00:09:55.710
preferences.

00:09:55.710 --> 00:10:00.560
We’ve got Whang and Pebbles, and we could
throw in SmarterEveryDay and Lindsey Ellis

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:07.240
and Marques Brownlee and Ssoyoung and preferentially
rank them all 1 through 6, and we still would

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:11.520
not get the YouTube government we all wanted.

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:14.150
SO WHY SHOULD I EVEN BOTHER?

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:20.020
And how much do you or I count, anyway?

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:25.230
In 1793, French philosopher Marquis de Condorcet’s
wrote that, "In single-stage elections, where

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:30.860
there are a great many voters, each voter’s
influence is very small.

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:37.660
It is therefore possible that the citizens
will not be sufficiently interested to vote."

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:40.050
Look at my plate of sand!

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:46.700
One single vote is really the inverse of the
Greek Sorites paradox where you remove grains

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:50.110
of sand one by one from a heap.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:51.370
Here we go.

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:52.370
Removing grains.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:54.529
One at a time.

00:10:54.529 --> 00:10:56.340
Grain two here we go.

00:10:56.340 --> 00:10:58.760
Grain thr-- alright y’know what this is
gonna take forever.

00:10:58.760 --> 00:10:59.760
Look.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:03.700
At what point does the heap stop being a heap?

00:11:03.700 --> 00:11:11.500
At what point does your infinitesimal vote
add up to actually mattering?

00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:12.870
PARADOX LIGHTNING ROUND.

00:11:12.870 --> 00:11:17.590
If a rational voter relies on their self-interest
to make their choice, the costs of that vote

00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:22.030
are usually greater than the benefits -- Anthony
Downs’ paradox of voting.

00:11:22.030 --> 00:11:27.350
Adding a new state can reduce the number of
Congressional representatives in another state

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:29.600
-- apportionment paradox.

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:34.410
Or a state with a fast-growing population
can lose representatives to a slow-growing

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.870
state -- population paradox.

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:39.630
Want to reduce the use of fossil fuels?

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:45.780
Green policies can incentivize a short-term
rise in consumption: Hans-Werner Sinn’s

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:47.450
‘green paradox.’

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:53.050
It’s so hard to make sense of all this.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:59.670
When I add up all the logic traps and complexities
of something that SEEMS so simple…

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:00.880
Drink or Eat?

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.160
Apple A or Apple B?

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:07.760
Hypothetically make my family happy or tell
the truth that I don’t want to Abilene and

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.720
neither do they?

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:16.460
The more I think about it, the less I’m
sure I know.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:21.970
Every time we make a choice like Whang! v.
Pebbles, or vote for a real president, we’re

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:29.320
subject to all of these hidden consequences
of decision theory -- and mine are different

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:30.720
from yours.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.430
You've got to think about all the options.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:34.580
All the scenarios.

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:37.750
All the contradictions and all the fallacies.

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:47.190
But ultimately, it's MORE important that you
don't get stuck in a cognitive web of indecision.

00:12:47.190 --> 00:12:51.800
Psyching yourself out from making a choice
at all.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:13:01.610
Or you'll end up staring at Bagel Bites and
Mountain Dew until you're a dead gamer donkey.

00:13:08.180 --> 00:13:10.090
And as always -- thanks for watching.

00:13:10.090 --> 00:13:12.960
I was gonna eat this but it’s freezing cold
so…

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:20.190
I am gonna eat it I’m just gonna heat it
up,

